artifact.self_contained_problem:
In the Standard Model of particle physics together with its expected high-energy completions, identify the following characteristic energy scales:
- the QCD confinement scale Λ_QCD ≈ 200 MeV;
- the electroweak symmetry-breaking scale set by the Higgs vacuum expectation value v ≈ 246 GeV;
- the neutrino-mass-related (seesaw) scale M_ν;
- the Peccei–Quinn scale M_PQ associated with a solution to the strong CP problem;
- the grand-unification scale M_GUT ≈ 10^{16} GeV at which the SU(3)×SU(2)×U(1) gauge couplings are conjectured to unify;
- the Planck scale M_Pl ≈ 1.22 × 10^{19} GeV at which gravitational interactions become strong.

The Higgs sector contains a scalar field whose squared mass m_H^2 receives quadratically divergent radiative corrections of order Λ^2 from any ultraviolet cutoff Λ, and stabilizing m_H^2 ~ v^2 against contributions of order M_Pl^2 or M_GUT^2 through a bare counterterm requires a relative cancellation of order (v/M_Pl)^2 ≈ 10^{-34}.

Provide a fundamental, dynamical explanation of the gauge hierarchy by answering all three of the following:
(a) Origin: Why are the strong and electroweak scales (Λ_QCD, v) smaller than (M_ν, M_PQ, M_GUT, M_Pl) by many orders of magnitude?
(b) Stability: Why is the smallness of v/M_Pl natural under quantum corrections, i.e. why does the electroweak scale not require extreme fine tuning of bare parameters in the presence of much higher physical scales?
(c) Desert: Why does the spectrum appear to contain no new particles and no new interaction scales in the vast logarithmic interval between (Λ_QCD, v) and (M_ν, M_GUT, M_Pl)?

A satisfactory solution should consist of a physical mechanism (or proof of impossibility) consistent with all current experimental constraints that simultaneously accounts for (a), (b), and (c).
